s in the woods and then we were  in bed by 10:00 sorry  and then the hardest part of the weekend  perhaps the hardest part of all the  research Sunday morning the camp was up  early as we packed preparing to depart  leaned and I heard sounds outside the  small travel trailer that we had been  allotted as our space to stay during our  time of camp we heard sounds outside our  trailer terrible sounds the sounds of  children playing one child about five  years old darted among the dormitory  trailers chased by two others perhaps  two or three years older the pursuers  threw curses and stones as they ran one  stone struck our trailer another hit the  youngest child he took refuge below our  window sobbing quietly don't hurt me he  pleaded upon being discovered  laughing the older boys pushed him into  the ground that forces face into the  dirt the biggest sat on him twisting his  arm up behind his back  I'm here to break your fingers he  taunted then I'll break your legs  put him in pain put him in agony  encouraged his partner I want to kill  him said the biggest yeah the other  agreed slit his throat he's a Jew he's a  Jew the breakfast bell rang it was time  for church for special service to  rejoice and give thanks it was Easter  morning  when we left Gail's ranch Sunday  afternoon I drove slowly half a dozen  miles my mind in jitterbug confusion had  all this been real did we hear correctly  did we see it seemed not possible not  believable could there be with us on the  same planet in the same time and place  such an affliction of spirit such  cruelty and loathsome corrosion of what  I believed it was to be human no of  course not  in a few more miles as Freud promised I  begin to build walls fantasies it was  all absurd I was sure a colossal joke  sort of embarrassing bit of social  flatulence I began to chuckle and then  laugh on down the road and and what a  joke it was funny very funny and allene  sat beside me a stone silent my mirth  increased it became shrill how hilarious  it had all been those sermons those  exercises that was a real good one shut  up Pauline said simply I continued to  laugh shut up she insisted I pulled the  car to the roadside turned off the  engine and still smiling  turned towards her and when she saw the  sardonic rictus on my face she was  gentle shut up she soothed quietly  please I looked over my hunched right  shoulder and my torso was still facing  straight forward arms held stiffly ten  in two o'clock tightly gripping the  steering wheel as that was I was as if I  was hurtling down the twisty mountain  road at high speed life and death in my  grip don't let go the corners of my  mouth strained to reach my ears pulling  pulling cracking my lips  straining all the muscles of my face  into a kind of rigid twitch  grin mask and there was pain from this  and I welcomed it my front teeth began  to dry I wanted to rip my face in half  and throw away the eyes and ears that  had seen and heard this weekend the  mouth that had agreed and asked and then  I wept bitterly uncontrollably aleem  drove twenty more minutes to a small  town and found a phone I was 42 years  old and a male but no man not for this I  regressed became childish I called for  help to one who was stronger than I one  who had been face to face with  homelessness and concentration camps and  McCarthyism and kept his humanity intact  please dad I asked what does this mean  how can we hate each other so much for  so little for a sliver of faith or a  shade of skin or our way of making words  all right thank you folks  Richard Mitchell is a professor of  sociology at Oregon State University  he's author of dancing at Armageddon  survivalism and chaos in modern times  it's published by University of Chicago  Press visit press news
 

